	**		CREATE ALTER, DROP, DELTE AND TRUNCATE -- all 5 commands are auto commit commands. We can not bring back the date once lost.
	
	** Deleting all the records from the table, the best option is TRUNCATE.
	
	TRUNCATE: DDL Command.
	
				SQL> TRUNCATE TABLE <TABLE_NAME>
				
		-- Once you truncate the table then there is no roll back (you can not undo it).
		-- Data(Rows) will be deleted. But, the structure exists.
	**	-- Deletes record and reclaim the space.
		-- Drop will delete the complete table including the structure.
		-- cannot use the where condition with truncate.
	DELETE:
		-- deletes records but, space cannot be reclaimed. 
		-- leave the space used for the deleted record. uses next row to accommodate the new record.
		-- Delete will delete all rows(data) from the table but, it can be rolled back as long as session exists. (TRUNCATE vs DELETE).
		-- To delete all the records from a table we have to use below command.
			-- once we exit from the session then it will be auto committed.
			SQL> DELETE FROM <TABLE_NAME>   (DML command)
			
		-- To delete a particular record
			SQL> DELETE FROM <TABLE_NAME> where <col-name> = <value> (condtion)
			SQL> DELETE FROM students where studentname='aks";
